  "query_preview": "/Through Wednesday/...\nIssued at 110 PM MST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nFlat upper level ridging will keep dry conditions in place through\nFriday, along with a warming trend. For tonight, mid and high\nclouds will pass overhead but some late night clearing and a dry\nairmass should still support near normal low temperatures.\nSunshine most of Thanksgiving Day and further upper level\nridging, warming aloft, and weakening inversions will aid high\ntemperatures warming several degrees above normal. We think most\nof the plains will be able to reach the 50s, coolest (near 50F)  \nin the far northeast and warmest (approaching 60F) in Denver. \nWinds will remain light. \n\nOn Friday, the flow aloft will strengthen as an upper level low\nand associated shortwave move across southern Canada and the\nNorthern Rockies, while an upper high holds over the east Central\nPacific. That means increasing winds for our mountains and\nfoothills, with gusts to 35-50 mph expected in wind prone areas.\nThe northern plains near the Wyoming and Nebraska borders will \nalso see winds increase with gusts to 30-35 mph. Fire weather\nconditions will be elevated due to the winds, but humidity levels\nare still forecast well above critical thresholds. Mid and upper\nlevel moisture will likely bring more clouds, and perhaps a couple\nlight late day snow showers for the northern mountains. Snow\nshowers will gradually increase in the mountains Friday night as\norographics and moisture levels improve, with the heaviest snow \nand more significant post-Thanksgiving travel impacts expected by\nSaturday. \n\nThat heavier mountain snow (and blowing snow) will be driven by an\nupper level disturbance racing off the Pacific and across the\nNorthern/Central Rockies on Saturday. We'll be getting into the\nleft exit region of a 120 kt jet, with improving QG forcing,\nstronger orographics, and neutral lapse rates. Therefore, all the\ningredients will be in place for at least a moderate snowfall\nevent. The only ingredient that would be more uncertain at this\npoint would be the moisture. Model and ensemble trends have \ngenerally been upward with QPF given the GFS and GEFS members \nwere also coming more in line with the stronger/slightly farther \nsouth solution. Several inches of snow are now likely for the high\ncountry, with potential high end of over a foot in the northern \nGore and Park Ranges. We'll likely need an Advisory for the \nmountains Friday night - Saturday night for the mountains, with \npossible Watch/Warning for the northern Gore/Park Ranges in \nupcoming forecast packages if the trends remain favorable.\nSaturday will not be the day for your post-Thanksgiving travel if\nheading through the Colorado mountains. Plan on Friday or Sunday\n(only lingering morning slick spots) for a safer journey. \n\nFor the plains and I-25 Urban Corridor, the depth and intensity \nof the trough offers up additional uncertainty in the forecast.\nGiven the favorable ingredients mentioned above, it would not \nsurprise us to see more widespread/numerous showers spread off \nthe higher terrain. The highest odds of this happening would be \nbehind the strong frontal surge on Saturday. If this can link up \nwith the forcing of the upper level disturbance, then higher \ncoverage and potential for banded snow would be possible. While \nmost areas would likely see limited accumulations due to marginal \ntemperatures, it should be noted that localized heavier bands of \nsnow up to 2-4\" would be possible (20-30% chance) in this \nscenario. We'll continue to monitor the forcing mechanisms and \ntiming closely to see if they can link up.\n\nBeyond that, dry weather is expected to redevelop by late Saturday\nnight and hold through Sunday night. The next chance of snow \nwould then start in the mountains by Monday or Monday night as a \nweakening disturbance pushes through the Central Rockies. The \nrelatively active weather pattern (at least for the mountains) \nwill likely remain in place with additional disturbances expected \nby mid week and another late next week. After a colder weekend, \ntemperatures are expected to warm back a few degrees above normal\nnext week.",
